<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>Peek.Worker</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Peek.Worker</AssemblyName>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <!-- Must stay false while InvariantGlobalization is true, or Windows speech (SAPI) is
         dead in this process. In invariant mode the default PredefinedCulturesOnly=true makes
         `new CultureInfo("en-US")` throw CultureNotFoundException; System.Speech builds a
         CultureInfo for every installed voice while enumerating them, swallows that, and ends
         up with no usable voice token - so GetInstalledVoices and every Speak call fail with a
         NullReferenceException out of VoiceSynthesis.GetComEngine.
         This is what actually made a packaged install silent. It looked like a trimming
         problem (the trimmed worker died with an access violation rather than throwing) and
         it isn't one: verified by reproducing the identical failure in a plain Debug build
         and in an untrimmed Release publish, then fixing all three with this one line.
         Setting it here rather than turning InvariantGlobalization off keeps ICU - about 30MB
         in a self-contained publish - out of the package. -->
    <PredefinedCulturesOnly>false</PredefinedCulturesOnly>
    <!--
      Land dev builds under Peek.Desktop's own output (also forced to ..\..\..\bin\,
      see Peek.Desktop.csproj) in a "worker" subfolder - matching
      WorkerConnectionOptions.WorkerExecutablePath's default of "worker\Peek.Worker.exe" -
      so a plain `dotnet build`/F5 dev loop finds the worker the same way a
      published/packaged layout does, not just in a happy-path published test.
      This must NOT be the same folder as Peek.Desktop's: the two exes' central package
      management scopes (src/client vs src/worker) can and do pin different versions of
      a shared dependency (e.g. SkiaSharp - Avalonia.Skia pins one version for the
      client, the worker pins its own), and a shared flat output directory only has
      room for one physical copy of any given assembly name, so the two would silently
      clobber each other. The literal "net10.0" folder name (this project actually
      targets net10.0-windows) is intentional: it must match Peek.Desktop's resolved
      output folder exactly.
    -->
    <OutputPath>..\..\..\bin\net10.0\worker\</OutputPath>
    <AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>false</AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Matches Peek.Desktop.csproj's windowed-Release gating. Redundant when launched by
       Peek.Desktop (WorkerConnection.cs already passes CreateNoWindow=true), but this
       also covers running Peek.Worker.exe standalone in Release, which would otherwise
       still pop its own console window since OutputType=Exe is a Console-subsystem app
       regardless of who launches it. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- NativeAOT is deliberately NOT enabled here, even for a self-contained Release publish
       (see build_binary.yml's "aot" mode, which is really just "self-contained" now - the
       label is legacy). This project uses System.Windows.Automation
       (AutomationElement.FromPoint etc., in Peek.Worker.Automation.Windows), which still
       relies on the legacy built-in COM interop marshaller - NativeAOT only supports
       ComWrappers-based COM interop, so the very first call throws
       System.NotSupportedException: "COM Interop requires ComWrapper instance registered for
       marshalling" out of AutomationElementIdentifiers..cctor(), and every call after that
       fails with TypeInitializationException (a failed static constructor stays failed) -
       i.e. this worker's core hover/inspect feature is permanently broken from the first
       mouse move. Verified in isolation with a standalone AOT-published harness calling
       WindowsAutomationService.GetElementFromPointAsync directly. Fixing this for real would
       mean replacing System.Windows.Automation with raw IUIAutomation COM interfaces via
       ComWrappers - not attempted here since AOT's only other upside (smaller size) is
       already dwarfed by LibVLC's own ~100MB either way. -->

  <!-- Plain IL trimming (not AOT - see above) IS enabled here, self-contained Release only:
       Peek.Worker.Automation.Windows sets UseWPF=true just to reach System.Windows.Automation
       types, which otherwise bundles the entire WPF+WinForms runtime (~40MB:
       PresentationFramework.dll, System.Windows.Forms.dll, System.Windows.Forms.Design.dll,
       etc.) into a self-contained publish even though nothing here uses any actual WPF/
       WinForms UI. Trimmed, PresentationFramework/System.Windows.Forms/.Design drop out
       entirely (nothing references them at all) while UIAutomationClient/Types/WindowsBase
       shrink to a few hundred KB each.

       Getting there safely needed two non-obvious overrides, not just PublishTrimmed=true:
       - PublishTrimmed's default trim pass disables "built-in COM" support outright (a
         documented, intentional trimmer behavior - https://aka.ms/dotnet-illink/com), which
         System.Windows.Automation's legacy COM marshaller depends on. Left at the default,
         every automation RPC call fails at runtime with "Worker RPC error -32603: Built-in
         COM has been disabled via a feature switch" - this is NOT a build-time error or even
         a build warning, it only surfaces the first time real code exercises it, so it's easy
         to ship believing trimming "worked" off nothing but a clean build.
         BuiltInComInteropSupport=true restores it.
       - Even with that restored, the trimmer still silently removed internal
         System.Private.CoreLib marshaling stub helpers (System.StubHelpers.*) that
         System.Windows.Automation's *is safe* variant only reaches through the CLR's COM
         interop plumbing rather than ordinary IL call sites the trim analysis can see -
         different UIA operations broke on different missing stub types
         (InterfaceMarshaler for GetElementFromPoint, MngdSafeArrayMarshaler for
         GetElementFromHandle/inspector tree traversal), so a narrow smoke test that only
         exercises one operation can look completely fine while a different one is still
         broken. Rooting System.Private.CoreLib (plus UIAutomationClient/Types, defense in
         depth) fixes this by keeping those helpers' reachability analysis conservative
         instead of removed. TrimMode=partial was tried first and looked promising (no
         warnings!) but turned out to just avoid the ONE code path in a shallow manual check,
         not the underlying problem - only running the real integration suite against the
         actual published, trimmed exe (Peek_TEST_WORKER_PATH) surfaced the GetElementFromHandle
         failure and the false confidence.

       Verified via the full Peek.Integration.Tests suite pointed at the actual published,
       trimmed Peek.Worker.exe (not just a clean build): 26/26 passing (one pre-existing,
       unrelated parallelism flake aside). A build succeeding, or even a spot-checked manual
       smoke test, is not sufficient evidence trimming this project is safe - re-run the real
       integration suite against the published exe again if these settings, TargetFramework,
       or the WindowsDesktop runtime pack version ever change.

       Deliberately NOT mirrored on Peek.Desktop.csproj: ILLink flags Avalonia's DataGrid and
       ReactiveUI's WhenAnyValue - both used throughout Desktop's UI - as reflection-dependent
       and not trim-safe, a materially different and larger risk surface than Worker's plain
       typed COM-interop calls. The two exes are published to separate folders (see
       WorkerConnectionOptions.WorkerExecutablePath) specifically so their independently
       -trimmed/untrimmed copies of shared runtime files never collide. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release' AND '$(SelfContained)' == 'true'">
    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <BuiltInComInteropSupport>true</BuiltInComInteropSupport>
    <JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>true</JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release' AND '$(SelfContained)' == 'true'">
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="System.Private.CoreLib" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="UIAutomationClient" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="UIAutomationTypes" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="PiperSharp" />
    <!-- Same class of problem as UIAutomation above, and it bit us the same way. System.Speech
         is a thin managed wrapper over SAPI's COM interfaces (ISpVoice et al.), reached through
         the CLR's interop plumbing rather than IL call sites the trimmer can follow. Trimmed
         without this root, the *first* SpeechSynthesizer.Speak call takes the whole worker
         process down with an access violation (exit code 0xC0000005) - no managed exception, no
         log line, just a dead worker. And because this is the fallback engine, it only runs
         when Piper has already failed, so a build can look completely healthy right up until
         the first machine that has no network on first run. Found exactly that way: a packaged
         install logged "Neural speech (Piper) failed - falling back to Windows voices", then
         the worker exited 0xC0000005 four seconds later and the app never spoke again. -->
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="System.Speech" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Peek.Worker.Contracts\Peek.Worker.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Peek.Worker.Automation.Windows\Peek.Worker.Automation.Windows.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Peek.Worker.SystemMonitor.Windows\Peek.Worker.SystemMonitor.Windows.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Peek.Worker.Tts\Peek.Worker.Tts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Peek.Worker.Tts.Windows\Peek.Worker.Tts.Windows.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Peek.Worker.Ocr\Peek.Worker.Ocr.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Peek.Worker.Screenshot.Windows\Peek.Worker.Screenshot.Windows.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Peek.Worker.Llm\Peek.Worker.Llm.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
